# The deprecated std names, as data: a std function, class, constant or module that a minor release has deprecated, which a later minor
# release may remove (docs/releasing.md, Compatibility: the standard library follows Python's rule). The compiler warns when a program uses one
# (kind `deprecated`, docs/errors.md; D129a, built like the unstable list, D121a). This is the one place the list is kept: to deprecate a
# name, add a row here and a row to the table "Deprecated names" in docs/errors.md, and a line under **Deprecated** in docs/changelog.md;
# tests/run.sh (`t_deprecated_list`) checks that the rows name existing std items and agree with that table.
#
# One name for each row, three fields joined by `|`:
#   name    `module.name` for a function, class or constant of a std module, or `module` for a whole std module (the warning is then at
#           the import of the module)
#   since   the minor version that first warned about it, such as 1.2 (the earliest minor that may remove it is the one after)
#   use     what replaces it, as a name or a short phrase ("itertools.batched"); empty when nothing does
#
# The rows of the modules `deprecated_fixture` and `deprecated_fixture_module` are test-only: no std module has those names, tests/deprecated/ has
# a file of each, and they are how the suite tries the warning while no real std name is deprecated. Every other row names an item std has.
export def deprecated_rows() -> list[str]:
    return [
        'deprecated_fixture.old_double|1.1|deprecated_fixture.double',
        'deprecated_fixture_module|1.1|deprecated_fixture',
    ]
